\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Synthetic benchmark: $n=1000$ assets, $T=2000$ i.i.d.\ Gaussian returns, five sector caps at $25\%$, $\rho =0.5$, $\mu \sim \mathcal  {N}(0,I)$. Lower panel adds Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage ($\alpha =1/3$). Speedup for the upper panel is relative to \texttt  {cvxpy} ($10.77$~s); speedup for the lower panel is also relative to the same \texttt  {cvxpy} baseline, as applying LW to \texttt  {cvxpy} does not measurably change its runtime (the ridge coefficient $\alpha \|X\|_F^2/n$ is passed as a scalar to Clarabel and the interior-point complexity remains $O(n^3)$ per step). The reported \texttt  {cvxpy} times include the $O(Tn^2)$ cost of forming $X^\top X$: the benchmark passes $X$ directly and CVXPY constructs the quadratic cost matrix $P = 2X^\top X$ during problem canonicalisation before dispatching to Clarabel. The Iterations column counts cumulative \emph  {inner} Krylov iterations across all active-set steps for MINRES and CG; \texttt  {cvxpy} interior-point iterations are not comparable (each costs $O(n^3)$ versus $O(Tn + mn)$ per Krylov step) and are therefore omitted. $^*$KKT uses a direct factorisation at each active-set step and has no inner iterations; the $4$ \emph  {outer} active-set steps taken are distinct from inner iterations and are not shown in this column. The small number of outer steps reflects the batch-promotion strategy: all violated constraints (long-only and sector caps) are added to the active set simultaneously at each pass, so feasibility is typically achieved in fewer than five iterations.}}{10}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:synthetic}{{2}{10}{Synthetic benchmark: $n=1000$ assets, $T=2000$ i.i.d.\ Gaussian returns, five sector caps at $25\%$, $\rho =0.5$, $\mu \sim \mathcal {N}(0,I)$. Lower panel adds Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage ($\alpha =1/3$). Speedup for the upper panel is relative to \texttt {cvxpy} ($10.77$~s); speedup for the lower panel is also relative to the same \texttt {cvxpy} baseline, as applying LW to \texttt {cvxpy} does not measurably change its runtime (the ridge coefficient $\alpha \|X\|_F^2/n$ is passed as a scalar to Clarabel and the interior-point complexity remains $O(n^3)$ per step). The reported \texttt {cvxpy} times include the $O(Tn^2)$ cost of forming $X^\top X$: the benchmark passes $X$ directly and CVXPY constructs the quadratic cost matrix $P = 2X^\top X$ during problem canonicalisation before dispatching to Clarabel. The Iterations column counts cumulative \emph {inner} Krylov iterations across all active-set steps for MINRES and CG; \texttt {cvxpy} interior-point iterations are not comparable (each costs $O(n^3)$ versus $O(Tn + mn)$ per Krylov step) and are therefore omitted. $^*$KKT uses a direct factorisation at each active-set step and has no inner iterations; the $4$ \emph {outer} active-set steps taken are distinct from inner iterations and are not shown in this column. The small number of outer steps reflects the batch-promotion strategy: all violated constraints (long-only and sector caps) are added to the active set simultaneously at each pass, so feasibility is typically achieved in fewer than five iterations}{table.7}{}}
\newlabel{tab:synthetic@cref}{{[table][2][]2}{[1][9][]10}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{sec:scaling}{{6.2}{10}{Scaling with problem size}{table.7}{}}
